  POSITION: Systematic-idealist reading emphasizing the unity of Fichte's
  transcendental philosophy and his philosophy of history.
  },
  keywords  = {Wissenschaftslehre, historical-experience, Grundzuge, five-epochs, Medium},
  abstract = {This paper sets out as its objective the exploration of some of the key passages and most relevant theoretical plexuses of the Grundzuge des gegenwartigen Zeitalters (1806) by Johann Gottlieb Fichte devoted to the theme of historical narrative and, more generally, of historiography. In particular, we will first of all aim to show the specific status of Historie. Secondly, we will try to outline the differences that Grundzuge establishes between the functions of Wissenschaftslehrer and those of Historiker, in an attempt to show, at the same time, the nexus linking the two and the activities they perform.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{klockonkolowicz2017egoismus,
  author  = {Kloc-Konko{\l}owicz, Jakub},
  title   = {{\guillemotleft}Wir alle werden im Egoismus erzeugt und geboren{\guillemotright}: {F}ichtes \textit{Grundz\"uge des gegenw\"artigen Zeitalters} als selbstreflexive Kritik der Moderne},
  journal = {Fichte und seine Zeit},
  year    = {2017},
  pages   = {234--254},
  publisher = {BRILL},
  doi     = {10.1163/9789004336674_017},
  note    = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Kloc-Konkolowicz interprets the Grundzuge as a self-reflexive critique
  of modernity rather than merely an external diagnosis of the present age. He argues
  that Fichte identifies the third epoch (of "completed sinfulness" and radical egoism)
  as the Enlightenment's own necessary self-understanding, which contains within itself
  the seeds of its own supersession. The "we" of the title ("we are all generated and
  born in egoism") implicates Fichte himself in the critique.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the second key question -- the meaning and status of
  "completed sinfulness" -- and offers the most developed account of why Fichte treats
  the present age as a necessary and self-undermining stage rather than an accident.

  POSITION: Critical-theory reading; situates the Grundzuge as a philosophy of
  immanent critique of the Enlightenment.
  },
  keywords  = {completed-sinfulness, modernity, egoism, critique-of-Enlightenment, Grundzuge, High},
  abstract = {In the paper I try to interpret Fichte’s Grundzüge des gegenwärtigen Zeitaltersas a reflective self-critique of the Enlightenment. Identifying the ›third historical period‹ of Fichte with the time of Enlightenment, I try to read his lectures as an ambitious and differentiated critique of it. The aim of Fichte’s devastating criticism on dominant characteristics of the Enlightenment (egoism, superficiality and self-interest) is in my opinion not to refuse it entirely. It is rather to continue it with an increased sense of self-criticism. The proposed reading enables to place Fichte’s arguments in the context of today’s debates over issues like the role of religion in civil society, or the meaning and fundamental values of Europe’s integration. For all his critique on modernity, Fichte refrains from treading a conservative, communitarian or collectivist path. This can be seen as the great advantage of his standpoint. What he intends is rather the »enlightenment of Enlightenment«, the continuous differentiation of knowledge and liberating the human being from the constrained form of modern consciousness.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{carvalho2020art,
  author  = {Carvalho, M\'{a}rio Jorge Pereira de Almeida},
  title   = {``The Art of Endlessly Taking Another View''},
  journal = {Revista Filos\'{o}fica de Coimbra},
  year    = {2020},
  volume  = {29},
  number  = {58},
  pages   = {431--482},
  doi     = {10.14195/0872-0851_58_6},
  note    = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Carvalho analyzes the "empty form of knowledge" that Fichte claims
  stands at the center of the third (present) epoch in the Grundzuge, focusing on
  how this empty formalism functions as both a unifying principle for the third epoch
  and a transitional concept toward the fourth. He examines Fichte's claim that a
  single "maxim of comprehensibility" can serve as the common denominator for the
  third and fourth main epochs, and reconstructs the connection to Kant's maxims
  of the self-preservation of reason.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a close reading of the Grundzuge's description of the third
  epoch (the present age), illuminating the second key question about the meaning
  of "completed sinfulness" and the epistemological structure of the present age.

  POSITION: Analytical-textual reading; bridges the epistemological and historical
  dimensions of the Grundzuge's third epoch.
  },
  keywords  = {empty-form-of-knowledge, present-age, third-epoch, Grundzuge, Medium},
  abstract = {This paper deals with Fichte’s The Characteristics of the Present Age, and in particular with his discussion of the “empty form of knowledge” he claims stands at the centre of the third – i.e. the present – age. 
  abstract_source = {s2},
Fichte speaks of a fundamental principle that forms the ‘common denominator’ between the third and fourth main epochs. This fundamental principle – the “maxim of comprehensibility” (Maxime der Begreiflichkeit) – makes knowledge and comprehension the measure of all that “counts as being valid and as really existing”. But the question arises: How can one and the same principle act as the “unifying concept” for two different “main epochs of human life”? Does this not go directly against Fichte’s claim that two main epochs differ from each other in every respect, precisely because they arise from two entirely different “unifying principles”, and because everything in them must reflect the difference between their “unifying principles”? 
Fichte’s answer to this question is as follows:  a)  the fundamental maxim in question allows for two diametrically opposed interpretations, so that each of them provides the principle or the “unifying concept” from which the third and fourth main epochs arise, and b) the third main epoch only gives rise to the empty form of science, as opposed to “truly real science”: it stands for a careless and easy-going, shallow, conventional, trivializing and incorrect conception of the “fundamental maxim of comprehensibility” –  so that it misses what is essential, does not do justice to the fundamental maxim, overlooks its implications, and indeed goes against its innermost meaning. 
Special attention is paid to the question of whether and how some major features of Fichte’s “empty form of knowledge” result from a misguided and superficial understanding of Kant’s “maxims of the self-preservation of reason” and can be reconstructed from this vantage point.},
}

@article{rueda2025correspondences,
  author  = {Rueda Garrido, Daniel},
  title   = {Correspondences Between Fichte's Worldviews and World Ages Based on the Fivefold Synthesis of {WL}-1804-{II}},
  journal = {Synthesis philosophica},
  year    = {2025},
  volume  = {40},
  number  = {2},
  pages   = {205--223},
  doi     = {10.21464/sp40202},
  note    = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Rueda Garrido argues that the logical necessity of both the world-plan
  epochs (as in the Grundzuge) and the typology of worldviews (Weltanschauungen, as in
  the Anweisung zum seligen Leben) can be derived from the fivefold synthesis structure
  of the Wissenschaftslehre 1804-II. He reconstructs the correspondence between the
  five stages of the WL's synthesis and the five historical epochs, showing that
  Fichte's post-Jena systematic works share a common deep structure.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the most rigorous recent attempt to show that the five-epoch
  scheme is not merely asserted but systematically derivable from the Wissenschaftslehre,
  directly addressing the key question about how epochs are deduced a priori.

  POSITION: Systematic-reconstruction reading; most recent contribution to the debate
  about the derivability of the five-epoch scheme.
  },
  keywords  = {five-epochs, world-plan, Wissenschaftslehre, WL-1804, systematic-derivation, High},
  abstract = {Na temelju peterostruke sinteze Fichteova Wissenschaftslehre-1804-II ispitujem implicitnu logičku nužnost i svjetsko-planskih epoha i svjetonazora u dvama djelima post-jenskoga razdoblja, u kojima Fichte nastoji objasniti povijest čovječanstva iz a priori plana (Die Grundzüge des gegenwärtigen Zeitalters) te povijest svijesti u njezinu usponu prema višem svjetonazoru (Die Anweisung zum seligen Leben). Svrha je ovoga teksta trostruka: (1) provjeriti odnos izme- đu peterostruke sinteze i svjetonazora; (2) provjeriti odnos između peterostruke sinteze i epoha svjetskoga plana; te (3) provjeriti odnos između točaka 1 i 2. Naravno, postoje ranija istraživanja koja razmatraju oba djela zasebno, no nijedno ne uspostavlja preciznu korelaciju između svjetonazora i svjetsko-planskih epoha. Ovaj članak nastoji popuniti tu prazninu.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@book{james2015republic,
  author    = {James, David},
  title     = {Fichte's Republic: Idealism, History and Nationalism},
  year      = {2015},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  doi       = {10.1017/CBO9781316275719},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: James offers a sustained interpretation of Fichte's political and
